Based on the details you provided for the 2023 tax year, here is a breakdown of how your situation is generally treated, what you can deduct, and the forms you will need.

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should almost certainly take the Standard Deduction.**
*   **Standard Deduction:** For a single filer in 2023, the standard deduction is **$13,850**.
*   **Itemized Deductions:** Your listed itemizable expenses are your charitable donation ($650) and potentially state/local taxes (capped at $10,000). Since these combined will be far less than $13,850, taking the standard deduction will save you much more money. 

### 2. W-2 vs. 1099-NEC (Freelance) Expenses
It is very important to separate your W-2 job from your freelance work:
*   **W-2 Job:** Under current federal tax law (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), W-2 employees **cannot** deduct unreimbursed work supplies or home office expenses. 
*   **1099-NEC (Freelance):** If your home office and the $1,120 in work supplies were used for your *freelance editing/proofreading business*, they are fully deductible against your freelance income.

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office (Freelance Only)
If you use the 180 sq ft room *exclusively and regularly* for your freelance business, you can deduct it using one of two methods:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. (180 sq ft × $5 = **$900** deduction). This is easiest and requires less record-keeping.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (180 sq ft / 920 sq ft = **~19.56%**). You can deduct 19.56% of your rent ($1,950 × 12 months = $23,400 × 19.56% = **~$4,577**), plus 19.56% of utilities, renter's insurance, and internet. This method yields a much higher deduction for you but requires you to fill out Form 8829.

### 4. Other Deductions (Above-the-Line)
These deductions reduce your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI) before you even apply the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. *Note:* This deduction phases out based on your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). For 2023, the phase-out for single filers begins at $75,000 and completely phases out at $90,000. Because your total income is around $93,000, you will likely be phased out and unable to claim this deduction this year.
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You will owe self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net freelance profit. You get to deduct 50% of this tax amount as an adjustment to income.

### 5. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, your return will likely include the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income (from Schedule C) and claim "above-the-line" deductions (like the deductible part of self-employment tax).
*   **Schedule 2:** To report your Self-Employment tax.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Required because your taxable interest ($1,880) is over the $1,500 threshold.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $19,700 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your $1,120 in supplies and home office expenses.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes on your freelance profit.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** Only required if you choose the "Actual Expenses" method for your home office instead of the Simplified Method.

*Tip for your state taxes:* Minnesota has its own tax rules. While federal law doesn't allow W-2 unreimbursed expenses, you should check if Minnesota allows any state-level adjustments, though typically they follow federal AGI rules closely.

